1
00:02:18,750 --> 00:02:24,750
The first Disney movie with attitude.

2
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:43,500
Oliver & Company is a animation, comedy, family
film directed by George Scribner.

3
00:06:56,250 --> 00:07:02,250
A young cat named Oliver is left alone in a kitten box,
while all the other young cats have new owners.

4
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:21,000
A big dog named Dodger shows him how to get food and later Oliver lives with him, his
owner Fagin and Fagin's other dogs Tito, Rita, Einstein, and Francis.

5
00:11:33,750 --> 00:11:39,750
Fagin has one problem besides being broke he owes a
nasty man named Sykes a lot of money.

6
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:58,500
If he can't pay it back he's in big trouble.

7
00:16:11,250 --> 00:16:17,250
While Oliver runs into a little girl named Jenny who becomes his new owner which he is
happy with and later Sykes sees Jenny as the key for him to get his money.

8
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:36,000
Oliver (voice), played by Joey Lawrence.

9
00:20:48,750 --> 00:20:54,750
Dodger (voice), played by Billy Joel.

10
00:23:07,500 --> 00:23:13,500
Tito (voice), played by Cheech Marin.

11
00:25:26,250 --> 00:25:32,250
Einstein (voice), played by Richard Mulligan.

12
00:27:45,000 --> 00:27:51,000
Francis (voice), played by Roscoe Lee Browne.

13
00:30:03,750 --> 00:30:09,750
Rita (voice), played by Sheryl Lee Ralph.

14
00:32:22,500 --> 00:32:28,500
Oliver & Company is a 1988 American animated musical adventure comedy-drama film produced by Walt
Disney Feature Animation and inspired by the Charles Dickens novel Oliver Twist.

15
00:34:41,250 --> 00:34:47,250
It was directed by George Scribner and written by Jim
Cox, Tim Disney, and James Mangold.

16
00:37:00,000 --> 00:37:06,000
In the film, Oliver is a homeless kitten who joins a
gang of dogs to survive in the streets.

17
00:39:18,750 --> 00:39:24,750
Among other changes, the setting of the film was relocated from 19th-century London to 1980s New York
City, Fagin's gang is made up of dogs (one of which is Dodger), and Sykes is a loan shark.

18
00:41:37,500 --> 00:41:43,500
In late 1984 or 1985, Michael Eisner and Jeffrey Katzenberg
held a pitch meeting with the animation staff.

19
00:43:56,250 --> 00:44:02,250
After story artist Pete Young pitched the idea to adapt Oliver Twist with dogs, the pitch was quickly
approved, and the film quickly went into production under the working title Oliver and the Dodger.

20
00:46:15,000 --> 00:46:21,000
Pre-production started with the crew reading Oliver Twist and watching the
musical Oliver!, developing the film for six to nine months.

21
00:48:33,750 --> 00:48:39,750
After production began in 1986, 300 artists and technicians worked on
the film within two and a half years of production.

22
00:50:52,500 --> 00:50:58,500
Along with The Land Before Time, Oliver & Company was
released theatrically on November 18, 1988.

23
00:53:11,250 --> 00:53:17,250
Oliver & Company received mixed reviews from critics.

24
00:55:30,000 --> 00:55:36,000
The music and vocal performances of celebrities were praised, while the animation, designs,
its use of computer-generated imagery, and plot received divided reviews.

25
00:57:48,750 --> 00:57:54,750
The film however performed very well at the box office, grossing $121
million worldwide against the budget of $31 million.

26
01:00:07,500 --> 01:00:13,500
The film was re-released in theaters in the United States,
Canada, and the United Kingdom on March 29, 1996.

27
01:02:26,250 --> 01:02:32,250
It was released on home video in North America on
September 24, 1996, on VHS and LaserDisc.

28
01:04:45,000 --> 01:04:51,000
A DVD edition followed on May 14, 2002, as a special edition.

29
01:07:03,750 --> 01:07:09,750
Oliver & Company was later released on Blu-ray Disc on August
6, 2013, to commemorate its 25th anniversary.

30
01:09:22,500 --> 01:09:28,500
The film explores themes of new york city, based on
novel or book, butler, cartoon, villain.

31
01:11:41,250 --> 01:11:47,250
The tone is lighthearted.
